1
00:00:00.100 --> 00:00:03.893
Most designers are handed a screen to improve.

2
00:00:03.894 --> 00:00:06.958
I get handed the thing underneath it.

3
00:00:06.959 --> 00:00:10.215
Six systems became one platform.

4
00:00:10.216 --> 00:00:14.001
Campaign planning went from two weeks to three hours.

5
00:00:14.002 --> 00:00:15.648
This is how.

6
00:00:15.649 --> 00:00:18.424
Five minutes, on real work.

7
00:00:18.425 --> 00:00:20.191
Five products.

8
00:00:20.192 --> 00:00:22.631
An ad-trading platform.

9
00:00:22.632 --> 00:00:26.741
Two LLM products reading financial documents.

10
00:00:26.742 --> 00:00:30.648
The operating system of a two-hundred-person company.

11
00:00:30.649 --> 00:00:34.001
And a learning platform for engineers.

12
00:00:34.002 --> 00:00:38.400
Every number you'll hear is the locked figure from the case page.

13
00:00:38.401 --> 00:00:44.446
Where a client is under NDA, the screens are labelled reconstructions.

14
00:00:44.447 --> 00:00:48.087
It ends with the part where I got it wrong.

15
00:00:48.088 --> 00:00:52.751
Because a process that can't show its failures isn't a process.

16
00:00:52.752 --> 00:00:54.374
It's a pitch.

17
00:00:55.702 --> 00:00:58.501
Everything starts with listening.

18
00:00:58.502 --> 00:01:02.451
And listening means being ready to refuse the brief.

19
00:01:02.452 --> 00:01:06.651
At a two-hundred-person company, the ask looked simple.

20
00:01:06.652 --> 00:01:09.663
Build the tools the managers need.

21
00:01:09.664 --> 00:01:12.138
There were no managers.

22
00:01:12.139 --> 00:01:13.813
On purpose.

23
00:01:13.814 --> 00:01:22.313
So every safe, obvious feature — approvals, sign-offs, escalation — had a manager hiding inside it.

24
00:01:22.314 --> 00:01:26.151
The design work wasn't adding those features.

25
00:01:26.152 --> 00:01:32.664
It was refusing them, one at a time, and finding what stands in their place.

26
00:01:33.730 --> 00:01:37.317
Structure is where listening becomes the decision.

27
00:01:37.318 --> 00:01:45.871
At Talon Outdoor — the UK's largest out-of-home agency — the recommendation model was already beating the traders.

28
00:01:45.872 --> 00:01:48.604
And they overrode it anyway.

29
00:01:48.605 --> 00:01:51.116
So the model didn't change.

30
00:01:51.117 --> 00:01:53.530
What it handed over did.

31
00:01:53.531 --> 00:01:58.016
A ranked list of billboards became a full campaign plan.

32
00:01:58.017 --> 00:02:00.173
KP Is attached.

33
00:02:00.174 --> 00:02:02.832
Customisable in one click.

34
00:02:02.833 --> 00:02:04.597
Same engine.

35
00:02:04.598 --> 00:02:07.268
Completely different argument.

36
00:02:07.269 --> 00:02:10.933
One a trader can stake their name on.

37
00:02:12.078 --> 00:02:16.506
Proving means the failure modes ship before the feature does.

38
00:02:16.507 --> 00:02:22.298
On the private-equity screener, analysts are paid to doubt confident numbers.

39
00:02:22.299 --> 00:02:25.157
So no score travels alone.

40
00:02:25.158 --> 00:02:27.236
Explain this score.

41
00:02:27.237 --> 00:02:30.689
And the number opens into its sources.

42
00:02:30.690 --> 00:02:32.459
The board pack.

43
00:02:32.460 --> 00:02:34.749
The audited statements.

44
00:02:34.750 --> 00:02:38.363
And the one claim that couldn't be verified.

45
00:02:38.364 --> 00:02:40.058
Kept visible.

46
00:02:40.059 --> 00:02:44.675
An honest I'm not sure, instead of a confident guess.

47
00:02:44.676 --> 00:02:47.360
And how I prove things today?

48
00:02:47.361 --> 00:02:51.135
My prototypes are working HTML.

49
00:02:51.136 --> 00:02:54.266
The site you're watching is one of them.

50
00:02:54.267 --> 00:02:57.533
Ask it something outside its scope.

51
00:02:57.534 --> 00:03:00.182
It declines, by design.

52
00:03:00.183 --> 00:03:02.843
Trust doesn't erode slowly.

53
00:03:02.844 --> 00:03:06.568
It dies in a single confident wrong answer.

54
00:03:06.569 --> 00:03:09.774
So the boundary ships in the interface.

55
00:03:09.775 --> 00:03:12.015
Not in the fine print.

56
00:03:13.190 --> 00:03:16.213
Now the part that didn't work.

57
00:03:16.214 --> 00:03:21.893
On PTC's accessibility pass, I did what felt thorough.

58
00:03:21.894 --> 00:03:26.349
Every element loaded with ARIA labels.

59
00:03:26.350 --> 00:03:27.790
Long.

60
00:03:27.791 --> 00:03:29.338
Careful.

61
00:03:29.339 --> 00:03:30.983
Exhaustive.

62
00:03:30.984 --> 00:03:35.480
Then I tested with real visually-impaired users.

63
00:03:35.481 --> 00:03:39.053
And watched it fail in front of me.

64
00:03:39.054 --> 00:03:42.355
They don't listen through sentences.

65
00:03:42.356 --> 00:03:44.026
They skim.

66
00:03:44.027 --> 00:03:46.838
By heading and landmark.

67
00:03:46.839 --> 00:03:48.292
Fast.

68
00:03:48.293 --> 00:03:54.569
My thorough labels were slowing down exactly the people I built them for.

69
00:03:54.570 --> 00:03:58.673
So I switched my monitor off for a week.

70
00:03:58.674 --> 00:04:03.727
And navigated the whole system by screen reader alone.

71
00:04:03.728 --> 00:04:08.578
Then I re-did the front-end with what that week taught me.

72
00:04:08.579 --> 00:04:14.992
The second version worked because the first one had failed where I could see it.

73
00:04:16.274 --> 00:04:19.498
Landing is measured, with baselines.

74
00:04:19.499 --> 00:04:22.872
Campaign planning: two weeks to three hours.

75
00:04:22.873 --> 00:04:28.879
Deal screening: sixty percent faster, measured before and after rollout.

76
00:04:28.880 --> 00:04:32.580
Technical diligence: three weeks to four days.

77
00:04:32.581 --> 00:04:37.107
Two hundred and fifty people running on one operating system.

78
00:04:37.108 --> 00:04:38.976
Zero managers.

79
00:04:38.977 --> 00:04:42.702
And four million people on a self-service app.

80
00:04:42.703 --> 00:04:48.588
That one is O2's number, publicly reported, and the card says so.

81
00:04:48.589 --> 00:04:54.535
Every one of these is on my homepage, with its measurement basis one click deep.

82
00:04:55.598 --> 00:04:56.942
Listen.

83
00:04:56.943 --> 00:04:58.478
Structure.

84
00:04:58.479 --> 00:04:59.728
Prove.

85
00:04:59.729 --> 00:05:01.135
Land.

86
00:05:01.136 --> 00:05:06.017
Then the loop restarts on what people actually did with it.

87
00:05:06.018 --> 00:05:10.913
I once argued hard for a three-option recommendation card.

88
00:05:10.914 --> 00:05:15.327
The live AB test made the single-option version win.

89
00:05:15.328 --> 00:05:18.830
I was wrong, and the test was cheap.

90
00:05:18.831 --> 00:05:20.666
That's the job.

91
00:05:20.667 --> 00:05:24.194
Six systems became one platform.

92
00:05:24.195 --> 00:05:28.582
I'm looking for one seat where that is the whole job.

93
00:05:28.583 --> 00:05:30.718
Not a phase of it.
